============================================================
DECISIONS
============================================================
D1  Piskel: useful, but not as a generator.
    - What it is: a browser pixel editor you draw in by hand (Apache 2.0,
      maintained; last commit 2026-10-28).
    - Its CLI only exports an existing .piskel to PNG, and it needs PhantomJS
      (dead). There is no drawing API. Driving its UI from a browser would be
      slower and less exact than the Pillow scripts the art agent already uses.
    - Its file format is plain JSON with one base64 PNG strip per layer, so we
      can write it from Python.
    - So: every design is also saved as a layered .piskel file. The Director
      can open it in Piskel to touch it up, and later draw the movement frames
      with onion skin.
    - Set up: built locally in local/piskel/ (gitignored, never committed).
      Run it:  cd local/piskel && node scripts/serve.js --test
      then open http://localhost:9001 and load the .piskel file
      (Open / Import). Or use piskelapp.com, which reads the same files.
    - Helper: tools/art/piskel_file.py (PNG layers -> .piskel), written by the
      art agent. Checked: a 2-layer, 3-frame test file loaded in the local
      Piskel build in headless Chromium (16x16, both layers, 3 frames each,
      pixel colors kept).

D2  Draw bigger: masters at 8x the game size for sprites up to 64 px on their
    longest side, 4x for bigger ones (the island). The master is kept next to
    each round as <asset>-r<n>-master.png.
    GDD 12 art_style updated; decision-log row 128.

D3  GDD gaps filled (`guess`): each of the 7 specs got an `art_canvas` row,
    the image size the design is drawn at. Decision-log row 129.
      meteorite      28 x 28, one still frame of the medium rock
      coin           24 x 24 per coin, a strip of two: common, rare (48 x 24)
      floating isl.  320 x 120, the rectangle, ground fills the box exactly
      fireball       64 x 64, flying down-right at 35 deg, head center (44, 44)
      UFO            40 x 20, disc in the bottom 10 rows, dome above
      planet         64 x 64, fills the hitbox circle; field rings not included
      electric ball  24 x 24, 16 px ball centered, arcs may reach the edge
    Small/big variants are the same art shrunk or grown.

D4  Agent edits (Director gave the go-ahead for the art/critic pair):
    .claude/agents/art-audio-engineer.md
    - 8x / 4x masters, master kept (matches D2).
    - New round outputs: <asset>-r<n>-master.png and <asset>-r<n>.piskel.
    - One script per asset (tools/art/<asset>.py), so parallel loops never
      edit the same file.
    - Staged CREDITS: one shell append per loop round, never a rewrite
      (parallel loops share the file).
    ui-art-critic.md: not changed. Its rubric already fits.

D5  /ui-art-loop has disable-model-invocation set, so the main session can't
    call it as a skill. The main session runs its steps exactly as written in
    .claude/skills/ui-art-loop/SKILL.md (make, measure, score, refine; stop
    rule; max 3 rounds). One run = one design.

D6  What counts as a pass: a run whose round meets the skill's stop rule
    (Score >= 8, Lowest >= 6, Size >= 8). A run that reaches round 3 without
    it is a fail. The passing round is the design.

D7  Asset names: <drifter>-d<k>, k = design number. Example meteorite-d1,
    files staging/assets/sprites/meteorite-d1-r1.png ...
    Each design gets its own direction in the brief, so the 5 differ.
    Each drifter has 7 directions ready (5 + 2 spare for fails).

D8  Pace: up to 2 runs per drifter at once, all 7 drifters side by side.
    A new run starts only while (passes + running) < 5 and
    (fails + running) < 3, so neither cap can be overshot.

D9  GDD 12 `glow` says hazards glow; the meteorite spec says "no glow". The
    art agent followed the meteorite spec (more specific). Kept that way.
    Worth one line in GDD 12 later: "except where the drifter spec says
    no glow". Not edited now.

D10 Refine rounds edit the same script (tools/art/<asset>.py), so the
    script only rebuilds the latest round. Every round's PNG, master and
    .piskel are kept in staging, so nothing is lost. Accepted.

D11 Watch item: the ufo-d2 round 2 refine shrank the saucer to 90% to make
    room for a glow, so the drawing no longer fills the hitbox. A hitbox
    bigger than the drawing means hits from an empty edge (Pillar 2).
    The critic scores Size; if this design is picked, redraw at 100%.

D12 Late briefs carry lessons. For runs started after a drifter already had
    a pass or two fails (floating-island-d4, coin-d5), the round 1 brief
    points the art agent at the passing reports and lists what the critic
    kept asking for. Same direction list, same rules; only a shortcut so
    fewer rounds are spent re-learning the same notes.

D13 Usage limit. Around 18:00 UTC the Sonnet sub-agents hit the account's
    5-hour limit (reset 18:40). Nine art agents stopped mid-task. At 19:40
    the Director said "CONTINUE"; the nine were resumed with SendMessage
    (same agent, same context), not restarted. Three had already written
    their files and only finished the checks; six drew from scratch.
    No round was skipped or counted twice.

D14 One critic prompt for planet-d2 round 2 named the wrong previous report
    (planet-d1-r1.md instead of planet-d2-r1.md). The critic noticed and
    used the right one. The score is unaffected.

D15 Electric ball stopped at 3 fails (d1, d3, d5) with 2 passes (d2, d4).
    That is the rule the Director set, so no more runs.
    Process gap found on the way: the skill's measure step passes only the
    image size (24 x 24). The 16 px ball inside it is never measured for the
    critic, so the critic capped Size at 7 on d5 for three rounds. The art
    agent did measure it (16 x 16 at alpha >= 50%). Suggestion for later:
    let the measure step pass the body size for sprites whose `art_canvas`
    is bigger than the body (electric ball, coin). Not changed now.

D16 Coin stopped at 3 fails (d3, d4, d5) with 2 passes (d1, d2).
    Note on d5: the critic scored Colors 7 three rounds running for a
    "magenta, not crimson" lit face, judged by eye on the board. The art
    agent's pixel samples of that face were (204,32,95) and (193,33,103),
    which is the spec's crimson (200,30,90). The critic can't measure
    pixels by design, so small-sprite colour calls can be off. coin-d5-r3 is
    worth a look anyway; it may be closer than its score says.

